###########################
#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserRadioButtons.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

TEXT(beginproblem());


###########################
#  Setup

Context("Numeric");
##L3 Q7.2
$radio1 = RadioButtons(
  ["Gina","Hassan","Both","Neither"],
  "Gina", # correct answer
  last => ["Gina","Hassan","Both","Neither"], # can be a list
);



###########################
#  Main text

BEGIN_TEXT
Gina and Hassan are confused about the definition of a boundary point of the surface. They know that the hemisphere \( S \) is parameterized by the function 
\[
F(s,t) = (\cos(s)\sin(t),\sin(s)\sin(t),\cos(t)) \quad 0 \leq s \leq 2\pi, 0 \leq t \leq \pi/2
\] 
and that \( F(0,0) = (0,0,1) \). $BR 
\{ image( "l3-boundary.png",width=>300, height=>210 ) \}

$BR

Hassan asserts: \( \textit{"Since the point }  (0,0) \textit{ is on the boundary of the domain of } F, \textit{ then } (1,0,0) \textit{ has to be a boundary point of the hemisphere.''} \)
$BR

Gina asserts: "\( (0,0,1) \textit{ can't be a boundary point of the circle. It's clear from the picture!''} \) $BR $BR

Who is correct? Clarify the relationship between the boundary of the domain of \( F \) and the boundary of the surface of \( S \) for Gina and Hassan.

END_TEXT

BEGIN_PGML
[@ $radio1->buttons() @]*
END_PGML

############################
#  Answer evaluation

install_problem_grader(~~&std_problem_grader);

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ANS( $radio1->cmp() );

############################
#  Solution

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The correct answer is [$radio1->correct_ans()]. 

END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT('MathObject version. Uses PGML.');

ENDDOCUMENT();